\subsection{Ablation of \name{}'s components}
\label{app:bottleneck-ablation}
\Cref{fig:ablation-full} adds three variants to the comparison of \cref{fig:ablation}, on the same process and with the same training.
Normalizing FB's target (\emph{FB, normalized target}) leaves its optimum unchanged and gives the same curve as FB.
Applying a softmax to the bilinear scores and fitting it by squared error (\emph{softmax + squared error}) recovers the full difference from three features, like \name{}; the softmax parameterization, not the cross-entropy loss, is what keeps the difference with few features.
Replacing the one-hot immediate target with a kernel over graph distance (\emph{\name{}, soft immediate target}) caps recovery at $83\%$, because its fixed point is a smoothed successor measure that spreads mass from cluster A onto neighbouring states.

